    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d339">

                <pb facs="0455" n="343" xml:id="pg343"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 24074/4.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Minister Calhoun to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1910-04-22"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">April 22,
                    1910</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Calhoun, referring to legation’s April 15, says Mr. Baugh<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d339-f1"/> reported under date of April 20 that Hunan situation had
                    not improved; that it was feared the trouble would spread, and that he had
                    telegraphed Americans to return immediately to Hankow.</p>
                <p>Mr. Calhoun says further that he requested Rear Admiral Hubbard on 17th to
                    dispose of naval forces so as to render assistance if necessary, and that he
                    [Hubbard] reported the sailing of the <hi rendition="#italic">Cleveland</hi> for
                    Hankow on the 18th to be followed as soon as possible by other vessels.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d339-f1" n="1" xml:id="d339-f1-content">
                <p>Vice and deputy consul general at Hankow.</p>
